    "title": "Development denied by St. Charles Co. commission - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Max Diekneite ,\n37 minutes ago\nST. CHARLES COUNTY, Mo. – The St. Charles County Planning and Zoning Commission denied a request by a developer Wednesday night to build a large subdivision off of Highway N.\nIt’s in an area neighbors say already faces serious traffic concerns.\nNow, the developer will have to earn a “super majority” of votes from the St. Charles County Council if they hope to turn their proposal into a reality.\nThe project, called “Trailside Farms” would cover more than 250 acres west of I-64 along Highway N near Perry Cate Boulevard, across from Wentzville South Middle School and Timberland High School.\nThe proposal was submitted by Kolb Grading of DCM Land VI, a development company based in Weldon Spring.\nClose Thanks for signing up!\nWatch for us in your inbox.\nSubscribe Now\nDaily News If approved by the county council, the neighborhood would include 385 single-family homes and 89 townhomes, along with eight commercial lots for small businesses.\nPlans also call for parks, playgrounds, trails, a baseball field, a community garden and an ice skating rink.\nStaff say that a small War of 1812 memorial is also planned to highlight the area’s early pioneer history.\nBut after hearing from residents and an engineer behind the project and following FOX 2’s recent coverage of the development proposal , the commission denied the proposal in a 3-3 tie.\nCouncilman Tim Baker tells FOX 2 the council plans to review the proposal at its meeting on Monday, Nov. 10.\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to FOX 2.",
